\f1\b\fs36 \cf2 \cb3 Neural Network using PyTorch\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 What this code does\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 Builds a proper 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 multi-layer neural network
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3  (not just a single neuron) using PyTorch, and trains it to learn the AND gate. Unlike your earlier codes, this has a 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 hidden layer
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 , making it a true "deep learning" style model, even though it's small.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 Line-by-Line Explanation\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb5 \strokec6 import\cf2 \cb5 \strokec7  torch\
\cf2 \cb5 \strokec6 import\cf2 \cb5 \strokec7  torch\cf2 \cb5 \strokec8 .\cf2 \cb5 \strokec7 nn \cf2 \cb5 \strokec6 as\cf2 \cb5 \strokec7  nn\
\cf2 \cb5 \strokec6 import\cf2 \cb5 \strokec7  torch\cf2 \cb5 \strokec8 .\cf2 \cb5 \strokec7 optim \cf2 \cb5 \strokec6 as\cf2 \cb5 \strokec7  optim\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 torch
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  \'97 the core PyTorch library for working with tensors (like NumPy arrays, but built for deep learning).\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 torch.nn
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  \'97 contains tools to build neural network layers.\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 torch.optim
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  \'97 contains optimizers (algorithms that help the model learn/improve).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 The Dataset\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 X = torch\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 tensor\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ([[\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 0\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ,\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 0\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ],[\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 0\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ,\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 1\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ],[\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 1\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ,\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 0\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ],[\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 1\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ,\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 1\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ]],\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  dtype=torch\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 float32\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 The 4 input combinations for the AND gate, stored as a 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 tensor
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3  (PyTorch's version of an array). 
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 dtype=torch.float32
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  just means "store these as decimal numbers."\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 y = torch\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 tensor\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ([[\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 0\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ],[\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 0\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ],[\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 0\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ],[\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 1\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ]],\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  dtype=torch\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 float32\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 The correct AND-gate answers, one per row, also as a tensor.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 Building the Neural Network\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec12 class\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  \cf2 \cb3 \strokec13 NeuralNetwork\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 nn\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 Module\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ):\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 Creates a custom neural network by building on PyTorch's base 
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 nn.Module
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  class \'97 this is the standard way to define models in PyTorch.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9     \cf2 \cb3 \strokec12 def\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  \cf2 \cb3 \strokec14 __init__\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ):\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
        \cf2 \cb3 \strokec13 super\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ().\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 __init__\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ()\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 Sets up the network. 
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 super().__init__()
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  just makes sure PyTorch's internal setup runs properly before we add our own layers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9         self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 layer1 = nn\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 Linear\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 2\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ,\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  \cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 4\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9    \cf2 \cb3 \strokec15 # Input \uc0\u8594  Hidden\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 Creates the 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 first layer
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 : takes 2 inputs (our AND gate inputs) and connects them to 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 4 hidden neurons
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 . Think of this as 4 mini-decision-makers, each looking at the same 2 inputs but forming a different "opinion."\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9         self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 relu = nn\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 ReLU\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ()\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9            \cf2 \cb3 \strokec15 # Activation\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 ReLU
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3  is an activation function (like 
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 step
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  or 
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 sigmoid
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 , but different). It's very simple:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 If a number is positive \uc0\u8594  keep it as is\
\ls2\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 If a number is negative \uc0\u8594  turn it into 0\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 It helps the network learn more complex, non-straight-line patterns.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9         self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 layer2 = nn\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 Linear\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 4\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ,\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  \cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 1\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9    \cf2 \cb3 \strokec15 # Hidden \uc0\u8594  Output\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 The 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 second layer
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 : takes the 4 hidden neuron outputs and combines them into 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 1 final output
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9         self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 sigmoid = nn\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 Sigmoid\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ()\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9      \cf2 \cb3 \strokec15 # Output Activation\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 Squashes the final output into a value between 0 and 1 (a confidence score), just like in your earlier sigmoid code.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9     \cf2 \cb3 \strokec12 def\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  \cf2 \cb3 \strokec14 forward\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ,\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  x\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ):\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
        x = self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 layer1\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 x\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
        x = self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 relu\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 x\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
        x = self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 layer2\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 x\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
        x = self\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 sigmoid\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 x\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
        \cf2 \cb3 \strokec12 return\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  x\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 This defines 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 how data flows through the network
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3  \'97 like a recipe:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Input goes through layer 1 (2 \uc0\u8594  4 neurons)\
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Passes through ReLU (activation)\
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Goes through layer 2 (4 \uc0\u8594  1 neuron)\
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Passes through sigmoid (final probability)\
\ls3\ilvl0\cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 Returns the result\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 Setting Up Training\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 model = NeuralNetwork\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ()\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 Creates an instance of the network defined above.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 loss_function = nn\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 BCELoss\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ()\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 BCELoss
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3  = Binary Cross-Entropy Loss. This measures 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 how wrong
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3  the model's predictions are, specifically for binary (yes/no) problems. Bigger mistakes = bigger loss value.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 optimizer = optim\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 Adam\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 model\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 parameters\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (),\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  lr=\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 0.01\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 Adam
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3  is a smart optimization algorithm that automatically adjusts the model's weights to reduce the loss. 
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 lr=0.01
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  is the 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 learning rate
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3  \'97 how big each learning step is.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf2 \cb3 Training Loop\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3 \
python\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec12 for\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  epoch \cf2 \cb3 \strokec12 in\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  \cf2 \cb3 \strokec13 range\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec11 100\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ):\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
    output = model\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 X\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
    loss = loss_function\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 (\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 output\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ,\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9  y\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 )\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\
    optimizer\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 zero_grad\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ()\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
    loss\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 backward\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ()\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
    optimizer\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 .\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 step\cf2 \cb3 \strokec10 ()\cf2 \cb3 \strokec9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec4 Repeats 100 times (100 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 epochs
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3 ):\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 output = model(X)
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  \'97 run all inputs through the network to get predictions.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 loss = loss_function(output, y)
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  \'97 measure how wrong those predictions are compared to the correct answers.\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 optimizer.zero_grad()
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  \'97 clears old gradient calculations (housekeeping step, prevents mixing up old and new corrections).\
\ls4\ilvl0
\f2\fs26 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\cf2 \cb3 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec4 loss.backward()
\f0\fs24 \cf2 \cb3  \'97 calculates 
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 how much each weight contributed to the error
\f0\b0 \cf2 \cb3  (this is called backpropagation).\
\ls4\ilvl0
